  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Blustery and colder rain showers/elevation \nwintry mix into early Saturday.\n\nLingering light rain and snow showers continue downwind of Lake\nErie and in upslope flow across the Laurels during the predawn\nhours on Saturday. A band of heavier precip was noted on radar\nacross southwest PA at 320 AM Sat, associated with low to\nmidlevel warm air advection and fgen forcing. As this band moves\nacross the Laurels between 4 and 6 AM, snowfall rates will\nincrease. Roads, and especially bridges, are likely cold enough\nto support a thin, slushy accumulation in the higher terrain. \nGrassy surfaces stand to pick up a quick half inch additional \nsnow with this band. Again, accumulations will be limited to the\nhigher terrain, mostly above 2000 ft. \n\nPrecip within this band is expected to be mostly rain as it\npushes east beyond the Laurel Highlands, and rates will also\ndecrease with eastern extent as fgen forcing weakens later this\nmorning. It will be another breezy day with wind gusts of 20-30\nmph for much of the area, and higher gusts in the Laurels. \nSkies will become less cloudy from SW to NE as the day \nprogresses and a small ridge of high pressure builds in behind \nthe departing trough.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Next chance for rain arrives Sun night into Mon\n\nThe narrow ridge of high pressure overhead should promote a \nbrief window of clearing skies early Saturday night, but then \nstratus becomes possible with an approaching warm front in the \nwest, and patchy valley fog will be possible to the east of the \narea with stratus. Sunday looks to be a warmer day compared to \nSaturday, but clouds will continue to increase through the day \nahead of an approaching trough. By early Sunday evening, rain \nwill likely be moving into the western highlands, then spreading\neastward through Sunday night. Model consensus takes the sfc \nlow through the eastern Great Lakes with a sweeping cold front \nmoving through the area on Monday, and potential for lake effect\nrain/snow showers later in the day Monday. Most likely rainfall\nis in the 0.25-1.00 inch range. Approx 50 pct of the membership\nof the NBM suggests wind advisory criteria (45+ mph gusts) will\nbe met in the Laurel Highlands with westerly wind gusts along \nand behind the cold front on Monday.",
